Details
=======
CodeCache
---------
* Contains the basic lookup table for <ip_addr, CodeBlob>
* CodeBlob can have several subtypes. The important one being nmethod 
  that contains natively compiled methods.

nmethod
-------
Each nmethod object contains roughly the following information 

* native instructions for that method
* relocation info (essentially jumps/call/transfers). Now these can be
  virtual/static/native/inline calls [1]
* debug info
* exception info

[1] look at the iterator / switch logic in nmethod::cleanup_inline_caches
    to understand how the relocation information is disambiguated 
    based on static/inline/inline cache calls

* Registration of CodeBlob (or say nmethod) with CodeCache 
  happens in nmethod::nmethod after the object has been 
  constructed.

* CodeCache::commit is the method and it takes the codecache
  lock and commits data such that icode can use it.
  
* It only does ICache::invalidate. It doesn't appear to be 
  either changing memory protection when writing to it 
  or using a separate VA pointer. Not sure why Dalvik
  is doing it then, the latter is so expensive

ICache
------
* ICache::AbstractICache in cpu/x86/vm/icache_x86.hpp and SPARC / zero
  handle I/D coherence. They re stubs on x86 and SPARC.

* Now there are several aspects to this. d-modifications need to happen
  at safepoints or holding codecache lock or some other lock, release
  of which provides the fence. Although its unnecessary on x86/SPARC
  since stores are not visibly reordered.

* The reader side, aka code, will need a reader lock to avoid load
  reordering. Unfortunately neither x86 nor SPARC do load reordering
  so there is not even a hook for this. Not sure what they re doing
  for ARM/POWERPC. Mind you this is necessary even if I/D are coherent.

* If you think again, Dalvik probably avoids this by doing mprotect
  which is bound to use IPIs to insert fences on all relevant d's
  so they solve coherence and consistency in one shot.

CodeHeap
--------
* Bottomline, theres just one mapping to CodeHeap and its RWX

* Use this changeset as a reference
  http://hg.openjdk.java.net/jdk6/jdk6/hotspot/rev/6bdd6923ba16

* Heres how codecache for storing instructions is reserved. As
  you can see its using ReservedCodeSpace so there is just one
  mapping and its rwx
  For Linux look at os::commit_memory. 
  Its RWX or RW

* Heres how its reserved to setup the CodeHeap
    CodeCache::initialize
      CodeHeap::reserve
        VirtualSpace::ReservedCodeSpace

* CodeCache allocate picks out of the CodeHeap
    CodeBlob* CodeCache::allocate
      CodeHeap::allocate


